# SelfHost Coding & Scripting Guidelines

## Project File Organization
- **App Structure**: Each service in `apps/` must have: `podman-setup.sh`, `*.container` files, `environments/`, `configs/`, and `README.md`
- **Script Naming**: All setup scripts must be named `podman-setup.sh` (not `setup.sh` or variations)
- **Environment Files**: Use `environments/` directory with `.env` files (never commit secrets)
- **Config Files**: Application configs go in `configs/` directory, mounted as read-only volumes
- **Documentation**: Every service must have a README.md explaining setup and configuration

## Shell Scripts
- Use bash, include error handling with `set -euo pipefail`
- Use consistent logging format: `echo "[$(date)] INFO: message"`
- Check exit codes, provide meaningful error messages
- Never hardcode secrets, use environment variables
- Add comments for complex logic, update READMEs
- Test scripts with `bash -n` for syntax, run in dry-run mode if available

## Container Definitions
- Follow naming convention: `<service>.container`
- Use rootless containers where possible (all containers run as non-root users)
- Set appropriate resource limits (CPU/memory)
- Mount volumes consistently with project structure
- Include health checks when possible
- Managed by systemd user services (not system services)
- **User Permissions**: Use `--userns keep-id --group-add $(id -g)` for proper isolation (see Security Practices)

## PUID/PGID Environment Variables
- **LinuxServer Containers**: Use `PUID=$(id -u)` and `PGID=$(id -g)` for file permissions
- **Do not force --user**: Let containers handle user switching internally via PUID/PGID
- **Dynamic Assignment**: Set PUID/PGID dynamically in podman-setup.sh, document in env files
- **Permission Issues**: PUID/PGID resolve most file permission problems in mounted volumes
- **Note**: For non-LinuxServer containers, prefer `--userns keep-id --group-add $(id -g)` over PUID/PGID

## Environment Variables
- Use `.env` files for configuration
- Never commit secrets or private keys
- Follow naming pattern: `SERVICE_NAME_VAR`
- Document required vs optional variables
- **No inline comments**: Comments after variable values can break parsing (e.g., `VAR=value # comment` fails)
- Use separate comment lines above variables instead: `# Comment here\nVAR=value`
- **Validation Required**: Test that environment variables actually work as documented (don't assume functionality)
- **Sample + Local pattern**: Every service must have `sample.env` (all possible vars with docs) and `local.env` (only overrides)
- **Sample env format**: Header with image/docs URL, `# ── Section ──` grouping, every var documented with default, secrets use `changeme`

## Port Allocation
- Use ports in 8000-8888 range
- Document port usage in service READMEs
- Check for conflicts before allocation with `ss -tlnp | grep :PORT`
- Update NGINX configuration for new ports
- **Internal Port Verification**: Ensure container services are actually listening on mapped internal ports
- **Port Ranges**: 8000-8019 (privacy frontends), 8020-8029 (Piped), 8030-8039 (sync/utilities), 8040-8049 (media servers), 8050-8059 (fitness/productivity), 8060-8069 (*arr stack), 8070-8079 (media add-ons), 8080-8089 (books), 8888 (AI), 8191 (internal proxies)
- **Canonical Port Reference**: `apps/nginx/README.md` is the source of truth for all port assignments

## Port Binding Strategies
- **Reverse Proxy**: Use `${LOCALHOST_IP}` binding (e.g., `127.0.0.1:8080:80`) for NGINX access
- **Direct LAN Access**: Remove IP binding (e.g., `8080:80`) to expose to entire LAN
- **Security Consideration**: LAN exposure increases attack surface, ensure authentication is enabled

## Security Best Practices
- Run all services as non-root where possible
- Use internal networks for service-to-service communication
- Restrict external access through NGINX configurations
- Regularly update container images and system packages
- Use environment variables for secrets management